<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Sea Bubble</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1720</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The South Sea Bubble was a financial crisis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1720 caused by speculation in shares of the South Sea Company, which had taken over much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a> in exchange for trading rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>. The share price rose about tenfold before collapsing in the autumn, ruining many investors and implicating ministers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Robert Walpole</a> managed the aftermath and became the leading minister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-sea-bubble.jpg" alt="South Sea Bubble" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MostEpic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_South_Sea_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
